      "writtenOn": "2026-08-18T18:12:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "on intel to avoid many pointer increments, i use indexed mode.  convert all but 1 pointer to differences from a base pointer, which does incb.  but use the differences as indexes?  neon supports that.\n\nantigravity suggests pseudo code would be like this:\n// Setup before loop:                                                          \nmov     %x[idx], #0                                                            \ncntb    %x[vl]                                                                 \n                                                                               \n// Loop body:                                                                  \n1:                                                                             \nld1b    {z0.b}, p0/z, [%[src_y], %x[idx]]                                      \nld1b    {z1.b}, p0/z, [%[src_u], %x[idx]]                                      \nld1b    {z2.b}, p0/z, [%[src_v], %x[idx]]                                      \n// ... process z0, z1, z2 ...                                                  \nst1b    {z0.b}, p0, [%[dst], %x[idx]]                                          \n                                                                               \nadd     %x[idx], %x[idx], %x[vl]     // ONLY 1 increment for all 4 streams!    \nsubs    %w[width], %w[width], %w[vl]                                           \nb.gt    1b                                                                     \n\nthats basically C style array indexing, but takes an extra idx register and the top of loop is indexed\nOn x86 I use pointer differences, so it would be like this:\n// Setup before loop:                                                              \nsub     %x[diff_u], %[src_u], %[src_y]                                             \nsub     %x[diff_v], %[src_v], %[src_y]                                             \nsub     %x[diff_dst], %[dst], %[src_y]                                             \ncntb    %x[vl]                                                                     \n                                                                                   \n// Loop body:                                                                      \n1:                                                                                 \nld1b    {z0.b}, p0/z, [%[src_y]]                                                   \nld1b    {z1.b}, p0/z, [%[src_y], %x[diff_u]]                                       \nld1b    {z2.b}, p0/z, [%[src_y], %x[diff_v]]                                       \n// ... process ...                                                                 \nst1b    {z0.b}, p0, [%[src_y], %x[diff_dst]]\n\nadd     %[src_y], %[src_y], %x[vl]   // Only 1 pointer update in the entire loop!\nsubs    %w[width], %w[width], %w[vl]\nb.gt    1b\n\nThe loop target is a simple pointer, which makes it easier to decode.\na single add adjusts the base, avoiding multiple incb/add\nno extra registers needed.\n\nThis is just a nit... no need to change it.  It continues to work ok on intel, as long as the sizes are all the same, so you\u0027ll find examples in row_gcc.cc if you want to try it (on a510)",
